1
00:03:49,396 --> 00:03:50,406
Oh, how'd he do that?

2
00:03:50,430 --> 00:03:51,741
I've never seen
anything like it.

3
00:03:51,765 --> 00:03:53,276
I never have either.

4
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:55,378
<i>Daniel Fontaine,
ladies and gentlemen.</i>

5
00:03:55,402 --> 00:03:56,946
<i>One of Vegas' most
popular performers.</i>

6
00:03:56,970 --> 00:03:59,415
<i>Star of his own
television special.</i>

7
00:03:59,439 --> 00:04:00,884
<i>Without a doubt
the Magic Castle's</i>

8
00:04:00,908 --> 00:04:02,952
<i>most illustrious alumnus.</i>

9
00:04:02,976 --> 00:04:07,157
<i>Daniel Fontaine and his
lovely assistant, Sandy Farrell.</i>

10
00:04:07,181 --> 00:04:10,193
Sounds like Danny's got 'em
all warmed up for you, eh, Rod?

11
00:04:10,217 --> 00:04:12,695
Yeah. Just like old times.

12
00:04:12,719 --> 00:04:15,498
<i>And now ladies and
gentlemen, please bear with us</i>

13
00:04:15,522 --> 00:04:18,368
<i>for five minutes while
we prepare the stage</i>

14
00:04:18,392 --> 00:04:21,771
<i>for our next act, the Castle's
current feature performer:</i>

15
00:04:21,795 --> 00:04:24,473
<i>Rod Greenwood.</i>

16
00:04:24,497 --> 00:04:25,842
Timing is still off in the spin.

17
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:27,076
I thought it was okay.

18
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,078
It's okay for here, not for TV.

19
00:04:29,102 --> 00:04:30,546
We need to rehearse.

20
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:32,015
Nine o'clock tomorrow all right?

21
00:04:32,039 --> 00:04:33,805
Hm. Sorry, Dan. I'm busy.

22
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:37,209
At 9 in the morning?

23
00:04:42,315 --> 00:04:44,160
That's it?

24
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:45,194
No explanation?

25
00:04:45,218 --> 00:04:48,031
I said I can't make it.

26
00:04:48,055 --> 00:04:50,466
And I say you'll be
here at 9:00 tomorrow,

27
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:52,902
and you'll stay until
the illusion is perfect.

28
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:56,339
I'm going to Miami
tonight with a friend.

29
00:04:56,363 --> 00:04:58,241
I'll be back by showtime.

30
00:04:58,265 --> 00:04:59,398
Promise.

31
00:05:01,334 --> 00:05:02,379
Who is he?

32
00:05:02,403 --> 00:05:04,481
None of your business.

33
00:05:04,505 --> 00:05:07,016
I said who is he?

34
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,840
Hello, there.

35
00:05:11,511 --> 00:05:12,722
What do you want?

36
00:05:12,746 --> 00:05:14,479
Your gentleman is here.

37
00:05:15,983 --> 00:05:19,195
Tell him I'll be right there.

38
00:05:27,094 --> 00:05:29,414
Great show tonight, Daniel.

39
00:05:52,052 --> 00:05:53,997
Ladies and gentlemen,
watch the balloon closely.

40
00:05:54,021 --> 00:05:55,632
One, two, three.

41
00:06:00,427 --> 00:06:03,706
Now, the card you merely
thought of is inside the deck.

42
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:06,108
I'm gonna drop
it inside the hat.

43
00:06:06,132 --> 00:06:08,778
Your chosen card, the
one you're thinking of,

44
00:06:08,802 --> 00:06:10,379
is going to rise
out of the deck.

45
00:06:10,403 --> 00:06:11,848
Are you ready? Here we go.

46
00:06:13,273 --> 00:06:14,417
Did you see it?

47
00:06:14,441 --> 00:06:16,786
That was great.

48
00:06:16,810 --> 00:06:18,988
He... He is pretty good.

49
00:06:19,012 --> 00:06:20,356
Here's one, here's one.

50
00:06:22,215 --> 00:06:23,314
You see that?

51
00:06:28,455 --> 00:06:29,432
Uh... More, more.

52
00:06:29,456 --> 00:06:31,233
Oh.

53
00:06:31,257 --> 00:06:33,436
Oh, would you look
at that. That's fabulous.

54
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:35,137
Oh, oh. Whoa!

55
00:06:55,982 --> 00:06:56,960
Wait, here. Here.

56
00:06:56,984 --> 00:06:58,394
Here. Yeah.

57
00:06:58,418 --> 00:06:59,529
Oh. Oh!

58
00:07:05,659 --> 00:07:08,771
Excuse me. I'm sorry.
Excuse us, excuse us.

59
00:07:08,795 --> 00:07:10,907
It's dark. A person
could use a little light.

60
00:07:10,931 --> 00:07:12,375
Oh, look.

61
00:07:12,399 --> 00:07:15,678
And who do we have now, Inez?

62
00:07:15,702 --> 00:07:17,313
A very handsome couple.

63
00:07:17,337 --> 00:07:19,015
And what are your names?

64
00:07:19,039 --> 00:07:21,984
Uh, Julie March.

65
00:07:22,008 --> 00:07:24,086
And you? Ben Matlock.

66
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:27,223
Inez. Would you kindly hold up

67
00:07:27,247 --> 00:07:29,192
something from either of them?

68
00:07:29,216 --> 00:07:31,296
Something personal,
from either of you.

69
00:07:35,422 --> 00:07:37,400
All I've got is my wallet.

70
00:07:37,424 --> 00:07:41,126
Uh... Uh, ha, let's see.

71
00:08:12,058 --> 00:08:14,236
Oh. Oh, oh.

72
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:15,727
Yeah. Mm-hm.

73
00:08:16,897 --> 00:08:18,240
He'll never get that. Mm-mm.

74
00:08:18,264 --> 00:08:20,643
Mr. Martel, I have an object

75
00:08:20,667 --> 00:08:22,111
that comes from Ms. March.

76
00:08:23,870 --> 00:08:27,417
I can't see it clearly.

77
00:08:27,441 --> 00:08:29,151
He's not gonna get it. Mm-mm.

78
00:08:29,175 --> 00:08:32,288
I will close my eyes and
concentrate on the item.

79
00:08:32,312 --> 00:08:33,312
You too.

80
00:08:34,448 --> 00:08:35,658
He'll never get it. No. Mm-mm.

81
00:09:04,511 --> 00:09:06,022
Wait!

82
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:08,091
It's coming through.

83
00:09:08,115 --> 00:09:09,358
And somewhere there's

84
00:09:09,382 --> 00:09:11,822
a bird looking for it.

85
00:09:12,185 --> 00:09:13,395
It's a feather!

86
00:09:13,419 --> 00:09:15,064
Both: Oh!

87
00:09:15,088 --> 00:09:17,188
That was great.

88
00:09:18,692 --> 00:09:19,936
I thought we had
him, didn't you?

89
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:21,070
I thought we had him too.

90
00:09:21,094 --> 00:09:22,774
Feather, huh?

91
00:09:37,611 --> 00:09:39,376
Sandy, come on over, okay?

92
00:09:43,583 --> 00:09:44,694
Sandy?

93
00:09:49,889 --> 00:09:51,333
Sandy?

94
00:09:51,357 --> 00:09:53,797
Sandy, look, I
want to apologi...

95
00:10:04,104 --> 00:10:07,050
You've actually
done it, haven't you?

96
00:10:07,074 --> 00:10:09,018
Thank you. This was a
good idea. That was special.

97
00:10:09,042 --> 00:10:10,520
Oh, yeah... Uh, Mr. Matlock.

98
00:10:10,544 --> 00:10:12,121
Huh? Dan Fontaine
would like to see you.

99
00:10:12,145 --> 00:10:13,122
Yeah?

100
00:10:13,146 --> 00:10:14,323
Come this way, please.

101
00:10:14,347 --> 00:10:15,713
Oh.

102
00:10:28,728 --> 00:10:31,195
We enjoyed your show.

103
00:10:51,785 --> 00:10:52,895
Oh, morning, Bob.

104
00:10:52,919 --> 00:10:54,318
Oh, Ben.

105
00:10:55,989 --> 00:10:57,100
Find anything?

106
00:10:57,124 --> 00:10:58,935
Just the usual.

107
00:10:58,959 --> 00:11:01,437
I'm almost done, if you wanna
go ahead have a look around...

108
00:11:01,461 --> 00:11:03,372
Okay. Over there, if you like.

109
00:11:12,005 --> 00:11:13,482
You ever seen
one of these shows?

110
00:11:13,506 --> 00:11:15,084
No.

111
00:11:15,108 --> 00:11:16,418
I always wanted to.

112
00:11:16,442 --> 00:11:18,855
Just never, uh, took the time.

113
00:11:18,879 --> 00:11:20,322
Hm. It's fun.

114
00:11:20,346 --> 00:11:21,858
It's a club.

115
00:11:21,882 --> 00:11:22,959
You have to be a member.

116
00:11:22,983 --> 00:11:24,994
I got an invitation.
It's a good time.

117
00:11:25,018 --> 00:11:26,195
Yeah. I know.

118
00:11:26,219 --> 00:11:29,365
My contact's been
strictly professional.

119
00:11:29,389 --> 00:11:30,767
How's that?

120
00:11:30,791 --> 00:11:33,335
A string of burglaries
of members' homes.

121
00:11:33,359 --> 00:11:35,438
Oh. Catch anybody?

122
00:11:35,462 --> 00:11:37,840
Uh, we think a small
timer named Jimmy Collier.

123
00:11:37,864 --> 00:11:40,443
Only he'd need a
contact inside the club.

124
00:11:40,467 --> 00:11:43,346
So far we've come
up dry on that.

125
00:11:43,370 --> 00:11:44,880
Hm?

126
00:11:50,076 --> 00:11:51,420
How you do that?

127
00:11:51,444 --> 00:11:54,290
Oh, I used to fool around
with this stuff when I was a kid.

128
00:11:54,314 --> 00:11:56,291
Ha. Well, hey.

129
00:11:56,315 --> 00:11:59,684
Uh. Well...

130
00:12:02,221 --> 00:12:03,966
Wait. No. Wait.

131
00:12:03,990 --> 00:12:07,369
This... Well... Hi.

132
00:12:07,393 --> 00:12:09,027
Hi.

133
00:12:10,229 --> 00:12:12,463
Uh... I got the call.

134
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:15,745
Oh, you've been...?
Assigned to the case.

135
00:12:15,769 --> 00:12:17,847
Oh. Oh, well you know anything?

136
00:12:17,871 --> 00:12:19,081
Well, a little. Mm.

137
00:12:19,105 --> 00:12:21,884
Now, Ben, you remember
we agreed to disagree.

138
00:12:21,908 --> 00:12:23,352
Oh, oh. I agree that, uh...

139
00:12:23,376 --> 00:12:25,121
We can disagree.
Yeah, if it falls that way.

140
00:12:25,145 --> 00:12:26,655
Well, it might very well.

141
00:12:26,679 --> 00:12:29,492
I, um, have an eyewitness.

142
00:12:29,516 --> 00:12:33,629
Who will testify that Daniel
Fontaine was the only person

143
00:12:33,653 --> 00:12:37,165
who came into Sandy Farrell's
dressing room last night.

144
00:12:37,189 --> 00:12:38,501
Oh, yeah, and he
was seen leaning

145
00:12:38,525 --> 00:12:40,068
over the body.
And she was killed

146
00:12:40,092 --> 00:12:43,305
with one of his knives. And
they had an ugly argument earlier.

147
00:12:43,329 --> 00:12:45,307
- And she was seeing...
- Somebody else.

148
00:12:45,331 --> 00:12:46,575
Yeah. Hm. Wait.

149
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:53,982
Oh. So, anyway who's,
um...? Who's your eyewitness?

150
00:12:54,006 --> 00:12:57,052
One of the magicians. Calls
himself the Amazing Zeigler.

151
00:12:57,076 --> 00:12:58,487
Uh-huh.

152
00:12:58,511 --> 00:12:59,955
What do you mean, "uh-huh"?

153
00:12:59,979 --> 00:13:01,090
Danny told me.

154
00:13:01,114 --> 00:13:02,425
Danny?

155
00:13:02,449 --> 00:13:04,493
Daniel Fontaine.

156
00:13:04,517 --> 00:13:06,829
Well, I figure if I'm working
for him, I'd call him Danny.

157
00:13:06,853 --> 00:13:08,964
Oh, so you're going
to take the case?

158
00:13:08,988 --> 00:13:11,228
Ah, I'm thinking about it.

159
00:13:17,297 --> 00:13:21,510
Um, you... You know
about, um, Sandy Farrell?

160
00:13:21,534 --> 00:13:23,646
I know she's dead.

161
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:25,280
Well, she was, oh,

162
00:13:25,304 --> 00:13:27,549
what they used to
call in the old days,

163
00:13:27,573 --> 00:13:30,185
"everybody's girl."

164
00:13:30,209 --> 00:13:31,420
What does that mean?

165
00:13:31,444 --> 00:13:34,356
Well, she moved around a lot.

166
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:35,825
Who told you that?

167
00:13:35,849 --> 00:13:37,126
Danny. MICHELLE: Hi.

168
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:38,694
Hi. Hi.

169
00:13:38,718 --> 00:13:41,097
Hi. This is an
interesting place, isn't it?

170
00:13:41,121 --> 00:13:42,164
Yes, it is.

171
00:13:42,188 --> 00:13:43,199
Yeah. We were just...

172
00:13:43,223 --> 00:13:45,134
talking about the
case, Sandy Farrell.

173
00:13:45,158 --> 00:13:47,003
Oh, yeah. I found
out a couple of things.

174
00:13:47,027 --> 00:13:49,438
There were three
men here in the club.

175
00:13:49,462 --> 00:13:51,407
That she? MICHELLE: Yeah.

176
00:13:51,431 --> 00:13:53,609
Arthur Wolfe.

177
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:55,111
The manager?

178
00:13:55,135 --> 00:13:57,813
Mm-hm. He put her on
as a cocktail waitress.

179
00:13:57,837 --> 00:13:59,448
And were they,
um...? That's the word.

180
00:13:59,472 --> 00:14:01,917
Hm. Then Rod Greenwood,

181
00:14:01,941 --> 00:14:03,619
his assistant for a while.

182
00:14:03,643 --> 00:14:06,522
Oh, and... And
they...? I suppose.

183
00:14:06,546 --> 00:14:09,191
H-how about, uh,
the, um, Amazing...

184
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:10,660
Zeigler. Zeigler?

185
00:14:10,684 --> 00:14:12,928
Yeah, I don't know if
they had anything going.

186
00:14:12,952 --> 00:14:14,663
That's... That's
what Danny said.

187
00:14:14,687 --> 00:14:18,200
And then he was last. Danny.

188
00:14:18,224 --> 00:14:20,502
But the night she was killed...

189
00:14:20,526 --> 00:14:23,394
she was gonna go
off with another fella.

190
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:26,875
And I'm not being chauvinistic.

191
00:14:26,899 --> 00:14:29,878
It's just that she'd
left a trail of them.

192
00:14:29,902 --> 00:14:32,013
Uh... I'll tell you...

193
00:14:32,037 --> 00:14:34,250
I'll tell you what
I really think.

194
00:14:34,274 --> 00:14:36,652
I think that this illusion...

195
00:14:36,676 --> 00:14:38,287
You know, the one that
we saw with sword...?

196
00:14:38,311 --> 00:14:39,689
Yes.

197
00:14:39,713 --> 00:14:43,091
I think that illusion has to
be central to this whole thing.

198
00:14:43,115 --> 00:14:45,727
Danny told me that
when he first met Sandy,

199
00:14:45,751 --> 00:14:48,631
she had the plans
for this new illusion.

200
00:14:48,655 --> 00:14:51,834
She said she had bought them.

201
00:14:51,858 --> 00:14:54,837
Danny told me he was looking
for a new finish for his act,

202
00:14:54,861 --> 00:14:56,572
and so here's Sandy...

203
00:14:56,596 --> 00:15:00,208
But he knew she hadn't
really bought the plans.

204
00:15:00,232 --> 00:15:01,209
Yeah.

205
00:15:01,233 --> 00:15:03,412
But he looked the other way?

206
00:15:03,436 --> 00:15:07,316
Well, um... yeah.

207
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:10,119
Yeah, but... But look...
L-look at it this way.

208
00:15:10,143 --> 00:15:13,222
Danny's already a headliner.

209
00:15:13,246 --> 00:15:14,890
Sandy has this illusion.

210
00:15:14,914 --> 00:15:17,025
She's looking out for
her own best interest.

211
00:15:17,049 --> 00:15:22,631
She sees that she can go farther
by letting Danny have the plans.

212
00:15:22,655 --> 00:15:25,133
And that's how she
became his assistant?

213
00:15:25,157 --> 00:15:26,435
Yeah.

214
00:15:26,459 --> 00:15:27,803
And Danny, uh...

215
00:15:27,827 --> 00:15:31,106
got involved,
like all the others?

216
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:32,808
Well, that's what he said.

217
00:15:32,832 --> 00:15:34,243
Hm.

218
00:15:34,267 --> 00:15:35,677
Looking good.

219
00:15:35,701 --> 00:15:39,215
Prosecution's case
is looking good.

220
00:15:39,239 --> 00:15:43,518
And you're right, it is
an interesting place.

221
00:15:43,542 --> 00:15:44,987
I'll see you, Michelle.

222
00:15:52,384 --> 00:15:54,396
Well, good news.

223
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:57,432
Judge Cooksey was a little
long-winded this morning,

224
00:15:57,456 --> 00:15:59,367
but he was easy on bail.

225
00:15:59,391 --> 00:16:00,602
Well, that's good.

226
00:16:00,626 --> 00:16:01,737
Did you call Tyler?

227
00:16:01,761 --> 00:16:03,972
Yeah.

228
00:16:03,996 --> 00:16:07,076
You used to fool around with
that stuff when you were a kid.

229
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:08,143
How did you know?

230
00:16:08,167 --> 00:16:10,679
I'm a mind reader.

231
00:16:17,644 --> 00:16:18,942
Pretty fancy.

232
00:16:20,946 --> 00:16:22,057
What was your card?

233
00:16:22,081 --> 00:16:24,193
King of diamonds.

234
00:16:24,217 --> 00:16:26,361
Ha, ha, that's great.

235
00:16:26,385 --> 00:16:28,830
You should be performing here.

236
00:16:28,854 --> 00:16:30,465
The manager
perform. I don't think so.

237
00:16:30,489 --> 00:16:32,367
The owners wouldn't have that.

238
00:16:32,391 --> 00:16:36,305
I bet they loved having
Daniel Fontaine work here.

239
00:16:36,329 --> 00:16:38,273
Oh, yes. Daniel could
call, the owners would yank

240
00:16:38,297 --> 00:16:40,175
whoever was scheduled
without batting an eye,

241
00:16:40,199 --> 00:16:42,478
and we'd sell out every night.

242
00:16:42,502 --> 00:16:44,579
Hm. How often did he come here?

243
00:16:44,603 --> 00:16:45,747
Couple of times a year.

244
00:16:45,771 --> 00:16:48,083
Practice a new act,
try out an illusion.

245
00:16:48,107 --> 00:16:51,087
You gave Sandy her
start here, didn't you?

246
00:16:51,111 --> 00:16:54,656
I employed her initially
as a cocktail waitress.

247
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:57,592
Were you upset when
she broke up with you?

248
00:16:59,352 --> 00:17:01,330
I don't know where
you get your information.

249
00:17:01,354 --> 00:17:03,432
If it's from your client, I
think you should be careful

250
00:17:03,456 --> 00:17:05,134
about accepting
it at face value.

251
00:17:05,158 --> 00:17:08,592
Nothing around here
is quite what it seems.

252
00:17:15,101 --> 00:17:17,435
Mr. Zeigler?

253
00:17:20,473 --> 00:17:21,939
Mr. Zeigler?

254
00:17:45,365 --> 00:17:48,043
I am the Amazing Zeigler.

255
00:17:48,067 --> 00:17:50,346
Something I can do for you?

256
00:17:50,370 --> 00:17:54,550
Sandy was genuinely
fond of our profession.

257
00:17:54,574 --> 00:17:57,419
Couldn't fault her on that.

258
00:17:57,443 --> 00:17:59,355
Uh, well, uh...

259
00:17:59,379 --> 00:18:02,924
Uh, what, uh, could
you fault her on?

260
00:18:02,948 --> 00:18:06,751
Her taste... in friends.

261
00:18:08,321 --> 00:18:10,733
Oh. So there was no love lost

262
00:18:10,757 --> 00:18:12,635
between you and Daniel Fontaine?

263
00:18:12,659 --> 00:18:14,725
None.

264
00:18:16,796 --> 00:18:19,641
Because he stole
a trick from you?

265
00:18:19,665 --> 00:18:21,777
Not just a trick.

266
00:18:21,801 --> 00:18:23,078
An illusion.

267
00:18:23,102 --> 00:18:25,047
A great illusion.

268
00:18:28,974 --> 00:18:30,919
Oh, the one where the
sword went through...

269
00:18:30,943 --> 00:18:32,187
You saw it.

270
00:18:32,211 --> 00:18:35,457
Yeah. That one...

271
00:18:35,481 --> 00:18:38,515
put him on the map didn't it?

272
00:18:39,585 --> 00:18:42,052
Yes, it did.

273
00:18:43,189 --> 00:18:45,867
Daniel claims Sandy stole that.

274
00:18:45,891 --> 00:18:48,603
And you believed him, naturally.

275
00:18:48,627 --> 00:18:52,274
What were you doing in
her dressing room that night?

276
00:18:52,298 --> 00:18:55,010
I was worried about her.

277
00:18:55,034 --> 00:18:58,780
I was standing just
off-stage, preparing to go on.

278
00:18:58,804 --> 00:19:03,419
Going through my act,
step by step, in my head.

279
00:19:03,443 --> 00:19:04,753
I had a clear view
of the hallways

280
00:19:04,777 --> 00:19:07,656
to the dressing
room the whole time.

281
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:10,893
Daniel Fontaine
was the only person

282
00:19:10,917 --> 00:19:13,329
who went into
Sandy's dressing room.

283
00:19:13,353 --> 00:19:14,985
Are you sure?

284
00:19:17,890 --> 00:19:19,724
I'm positive.

285
00:19:23,229 --> 00:19:24,229
All right.

286
00:19:49,589 --> 00:19:52,189
Well. It was nice doing
business with you.

287
00:19:56,529 --> 00:20:00,030
Your dance card full, pal?

288
00:20:01,968 --> 00:20:03,667
Well, who's asking?

289
00:20:08,841 --> 00:20:10,585
Ben Franklin. Eight ball.

290
00:20:10,609 --> 00:20:12,509
Rack 'em up.

291
00:20:17,616 --> 00:20:19,082
You think so? MAN 2: No.

292
00:20:24,123 --> 00:20:26,702
Gonna play? Talk
or play? Come on.

293
00:20:26,726 --> 00:20:28,659
Fifteen, side pocket.

294
00:20:31,330 --> 00:20:32,330
Nice.

295
00:20:40,606 --> 00:20:42,005
Nice. Nice.

296
00:20:46,245 --> 00:20:48,813
Okay, okay, okay.
I'll buy. I'll buy.

297
00:20:51,283 --> 00:20:53,117
Eight ball. Right here.

298
00:21:02,028 --> 00:21:04,629
Pay me,<i> Jimmy.</i>

299
00:21:08,167 --> 00:21:10,607
How did you know my name?

300
00:21:11,671 --> 00:21:13,904
I make it a point to
know my competition.

301
00:21:16,876 --> 00:21:18,153
And I'm not talking about pool.

302
00:21:18,177 --> 00:21:19,688
What are you talking about?

303
00:21:19,712 --> 00:21:22,124
Your little gig with
the Magic Castle.

304
00:21:22,148 --> 00:21:24,793
Well, I'm sorry, you lost me.

305
00:21:24,817 --> 00:21:26,895
Come on, use your brains, Jimmy.

306
00:21:26,919 --> 00:21:28,597
If I know about your scam,

307
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:31,101
how far off the trail do
you think the cops are?

308
00:21:31,924 --> 00:21:34,669
Now you wanna throw
'em a curve, I'm your man.

309
00:21:34,693 --> 00:21:37,939
Subcontract a few of the
jobs to me, we'll split the take.

310
00:21:37,963 --> 00:21:39,508
I need the bread.

311
00:21:39,532 --> 00:21:42,944
You need the alibi.

312
00:21:42,968 --> 00:21:46,348
Let me make a phone call,
and maybe we can get something

313
00:21:46,372 --> 00:21:47,782
on for later on this week.

314
00:21:47,806 --> 00:21:50,485
"We?"

315
00:21:50,509 --> 00:21:52,554
Uh-uh. I work alone, pal.

316
00:21:52,578 --> 00:21:55,123
Hey, look. I don't even
know your name,<i> pal,</i>

317
00:21:55,147 --> 00:21:56,458
let alone how good you are.

318
00:21:56,482 --> 00:21:57,792
First, we work together.

319
00:21:57,816 --> 00:22:01,585
If I like what I
see, you got a deal.

320
00:22:07,026 --> 00:22:09,238
I think we were over
there somewhere.

321
00:22:09,262 --> 00:22:10,572
Right there. Yeah.

322
00:22:10,596 --> 00:22:12,374
This is some place, isn't it?

323
00:22:12,398 --> 00:22:13,718
Yes. Heh.

324
00:22:17,203 --> 00:22:20,883
My grandmother had
an old trunk like that.

325
00:22:20,907 --> 00:22:22,150
What was in it?

326
00:22:22,174 --> 00:22:24,286
I don't know. I was
too scared to look.

327
00:22:24,310 --> 00:22:25,342
Ha-ha.

328
00:22:26,813 --> 00:22:29,293
What do you
suppose is in this one?

329
00:22:36,021 --> 00:22:37,766
Do you mind?

330
00:22:37,790 --> 00:22:40,168
I'm sorry. We didn't know
anybody was in there.

331
00:22:40,192 --> 00:22:41,903
Just... Just close the
lid, would you please.

332
00:22:41,927 --> 00:22:43,005
I'm trying to rehearse.

333
00:22:43,029 --> 00:22:44,005
Of course.

334
00:22:44,029 --> 00:22:45,974
Ex... Excuse us.

335
00:22:45,998 --> 00:22:47,498
We're sorry.

336
00:22:49,001 --> 00:22:51,580
I'm sure glad I didn't open
my grandmother's trunk.

337
00:22:51,604 --> 00:22:53,644
Yeah.

338
00:22:54,974 --> 00:22:57,741
Uh... Uh...

339
00:22:58,911 --> 00:23:01,222
You go ahead. I'm gonna...

340
00:23:01,246 --> 00:23:02,524
hang around a while.

341
00:23:02,548 --> 00:23:04,247
Okay?

342
00:23:28,173 --> 00:23:29,584
Hi.

343
00:23:29,608 --> 00:23:30,852
How'd it go?

344
00:23:30,876 --> 00:23:31,853
How'd what go?

345
00:23:31,877 --> 00:23:32,921
Rehearsal?

346
00:23:32,945 --> 00:23:35,657
The stage area is
closed to visitors.

347
00:23:35,681 --> 00:23:36,925
Oh, I... I'm not a visitor.

348
00:23:36,949 --> 00:23:38,961
I'm Daniel Fontaine's lawyer.

349
00:23:38,985 --> 00:23:40,996
Oh. What is it you want?

350
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:44,332
Well, I'd like to ask you some
questions about Sandy Farrell.

351
00:23:44,356 --> 00:23:46,401
I understand that she
was your assistant.

352
00:23:46,425 --> 00:23:48,403
That's right.

353
00:23:48,427 --> 00:23:50,138
Well, when she...?

354
00:23:50,162 --> 00:23:52,306
When she quit you to
be Danny's assistant,

355
00:23:52,330 --> 00:23:53,575
how did you take that?

356
00:23:53,599 --> 00:23:55,309
Look, I didn't have
any reason to kill her,

357
00:23:55,333 --> 00:23:57,045
if that's what
you're getting at.

358
00:23:57,069 --> 00:24:01,984
Well, when she and
Daniel hit the big time,

359
00:24:02,008 --> 00:24:03,218
how did you feel?

360
00:24:03,242 --> 00:24:04,786
I felt fine.

361
00:24:04,810 --> 00:24:07,422
I won't lie. I'm sure that
everybody wants to be in,

362
00:24:07,446 --> 00:24:09,424
as you say, "the big time."

363
00:24:09,448 --> 00:24:12,995
Yeah. How about
when Daniel would just,

364
00:24:13,019 --> 00:24:14,997
out of the blue,
show up to try...

365
00:24:15,021 --> 00:24:16,798
Try out a new illusion.

366
00:24:16,822 --> 00:24:19,601
Would your act ever get bumped?

367
00:24:19,625 --> 00:24:21,502
Look, the fact is that
when Sandy came off-stage

368
00:24:21,526 --> 00:24:23,604
that night, I went on.
And I didn't come off

369
00:24:23,628 --> 00:24:25,106
until after she was murdered.

370
00:24:25,130 --> 00:24:28,109
Was your...? This trunk
part of your act that night?

371
00:24:28,133 --> 00:24:29,510
It's part of my act every night.

372
00:24:29,534 --> 00:24:31,679
Look, I'm sorry, but I've
really gotta get going.

373
00:24:31,703 --> 00:24:33,415
Oh. Oh.

374
00:24:33,439 --> 00:24:34,783
All right. Okay.

375
00:24:34,807 --> 00:24:36,318
Uh, thanks. Mm-hm.

376
00:24:36,342 --> 00:24:37,407
Okay.

377
00:24:50,790 --> 00:24:52,167
Any luck?

378
00:24:52,191 --> 00:24:53,902
Not so far.

379
00:24:53,926 --> 00:24:55,570
Hey, Danny, take
a look at this trunk.

380
00:24:55,594 --> 00:24:57,739
Is there any way that
he could have gotten out

381
00:24:57,763 --> 00:24:59,496
of this during his act?

382
00:25:03,469 --> 00:25:05,147
No. Definitely not.

383
00:25:05,171 --> 00:25:06,515
It's solid. Huh.

384
00:25:06,539 --> 00:25:09,083
Well, that leaves the
manager and Mr. Zeigler.

385
00:25:09,107 --> 00:25:10,084
Hm.

386
00:25:10,108 --> 00:25:12,019
What do you think?

387
00:25:12,043 --> 00:25:15,390
Well... I guess
we're back to the old

388
00:25:15,414 --> 00:25:19,048
needing a rabbit out
of the hat, aren't we?

389
00:25:24,456 --> 00:25:26,300
If I were you,
I'd skip the silver,

390
00:25:26,324 --> 00:25:27,902
head straight for the bedroom.

391
00:25:27,926 --> 00:25:30,486
Word is they're both
heavily into jewelry.

392
00:25:31,463 --> 00:25:33,075
You're sure there's nobody home?

393
00:25:33,099 --> 00:25:36,511
Positive. Well,
you sound nervous.

394
00:25:36,535 --> 00:25:38,947
We got all night working off
of secondhand information.

395
00:25:38,971 --> 00:25:41,950
Now, whoever this contact
of yours is at the Magic Castle,

396
00:25:41,974 --> 00:25:44,919
the next time I'll talk to
him in person, you got that?

397
00:25:44,943 --> 00:25:46,455
Hey, you haven't
proved to me yet

398
00:25:46,479 --> 00:25:48,719
there's even gonna
be a next time.

399
00:25:49,381 --> 00:25:52,549
Give me about... seven minutes.

400
00:26:31,690 --> 00:26:34,302
<i>This is the police.
Stop where you are.</i>

401
00:26:34,326 --> 00:26:35,337
<i>Put your hands in the air.</i>

402
00:26:35,361 --> 00:26:37,172
Whoa! All right, all right.

403
00:26:37,196 --> 00:26:39,529
Wait! All right.

404
00:26:51,110 --> 00:26:54,489
In this case, as in
any other murder case,

405
00:26:54,513 --> 00:26:57,525
the burden of proof
lies with the prosecution.

406
00:26:57,549 --> 00:27:00,228
We must prove beyond
a reasonable doubt

407
00:27:00,252 --> 00:27:04,399
that Daniel Fontaine
is guilty of the crime

408
00:27:04,423 --> 00:27:06,635
of which he stands accused.

409
00:27:06,659 --> 00:27:10,772
We must prove that, and we will.

410
00:27:10,796 --> 00:27:14,209
Because the evidence in
this case is so overwhelming,

411
00:27:14,233 --> 00:27:20,015
it is so compelling, that we
will establish Daniel Fontaine's

412
00:27:20,039 --> 00:27:25,654
<i>guilt</i> beyond any doubt.

413
00:27:25,678 --> 00:27:27,177
Thank you.

414
00:27:28,547 --> 00:27:29,947
Mr. Matlock.

415
00:27:34,153 --> 00:27:35,796
Members of the jury,

416
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:38,966
the defense is
under no obligation

417
00:27:38,990 --> 00:27:43,171
to make an opening
statement, and ordinarily I do not.

418
00:27:43,195 --> 00:27:46,974
But the events of this case
has given the prosecution

419
00:27:46,998 --> 00:27:50,611
a murder weapon,
fingerprints, a motive.

420
00:27:50,635 --> 00:27:53,581
And under the circumstances
that evidence would lead us

421
00:27:53,605 --> 00:27:56,639
to an ordinary conclusion.

422
00:27:58,043 --> 00:28:02,723
But Sandy Farrell was
murdered in a house of magic,

423
00:28:02,747 --> 00:28:06,528
where virtually
nothing is as it seems.

424
00:28:06,552 --> 00:28:10,698
Women appear to float in midair.

425
00:28:10,722 --> 00:28:15,736
Men appear to be
cut in half with saws,

426
00:28:15,760 --> 00:28:21,165
mentalists... appear
to read one's mind.

427
00:28:22,268 --> 00:28:24,912
And this man
appears to be guilty,

428
00:28:24,936 --> 00:28:27,437
when in fact he's not.

429
00:28:28,807 --> 00:28:31,119
And did you discover
the defendant

430
00:28:31,143 --> 00:28:33,054
standing over the victim's body?

431
00:28:33,078 --> 00:28:34,189
That's right.

432
00:28:34,213 --> 00:28:36,791
What were you doing
standing in the wings

433
00:28:36,815 --> 00:28:38,827
on the night of the murder?

434
00:28:38,851 --> 00:28:40,662
The same thing I do
every night before going on:

435
00:28:40,686 --> 00:28:42,097
preparing.

436
00:28:42,121 --> 00:28:45,133
Thinking about what I'm going
to say and do once on-stage.

437
00:28:45,157 --> 00:28:49,438
Mr. Zeigler, did you have
a clear view of the door

438
00:28:49,462 --> 00:28:53,841
to Sandy Farrell's dressing
room from 9 until 9:30?

439
00:28:53,865 --> 00:28:56,511
From the time she
came off the stage

440
00:28:56,535 --> 00:28:59,181
until the time she
was found murdered?

441
00:28:59,205 --> 00:29:00,748
Yes, I did.

442
00:29:00,772 --> 00:29:03,552
And Daniel Fontaine was the
only person who entered the room

443
00:29:03,576 --> 00:29:05,654
during that time.

444
00:29:05,678 --> 00:29:08,156
No further question.

445
00:29:08,180 --> 00:29:10,660
Mr. Matlock.

446
00:29:10,950 --> 00:29:12,950
I got this guy.

447
00:29:15,954 --> 00:29:21,035
Maybe you left for a
second or turned your back?

448
00:29:21,059 --> 00:29:22,737
That did not happen.

449
00:29:22,761 --> 00:29:24,305
I know what I saw.

450
00:29:24,329 --> 00:29:26,875
No one else went
through the door.

451
00:29:26,899 --> 00:29:29,744
Not one person? No.

452
00:29:29,768 --> 00:29:32,447
Uh, Your Honor,
pursuant to subpoena,

453
00:29:32,471 --> 00:29:34,115
uh, the defense
has received various

454
00:29:34,139 --> 00:29:35,750
pharmaceutical receipts,

455
00:29:35,774 --> 00:29:38,686
including one dated April 23rd

456
00:29:38,710 --> 00:29:40,522
made out to Sandra Farrell.

457
00:29:40,546 --> 00:29:44,092
And I'd like that identified
as Defense Exhibit A.

458
00:29:44,116 --> 00:29:45,860
I have no objection, Your Honor.

459
00:29:45,884 --> 00:29:47,028
So marked.

460
00:29:47,052 --> 00:29:48,195
Okay.

461
00:29:48,219 --> 00:29:50,431
Sir, showing you
Defense Exhibit A,

462
00:29:50,455 --> 00:29:53,802
April 23rd was the day
of the murder, was it not?

463
00:29:53,826 --> 00:29:55,337
You know it was.

464
00:29:55,361 --> 00:29:57,505
I'll take that as
a yes response.

465
00:29:57,529 --> 00:30:01,342
Now, this delivery receipt to
Miss Farrell's dressing room

466
00:30:01,366 --> 00:30:03,912
shows a time at the bottom.

467
00:30:03,936 --> 00:30:06,003
Please tell us what time it was.

468
00:30:08,106 --> 00:30:09,083
9:08.

469
00:30:09,107 --> 00:30:11,520
Say again.

470
00:30:11,544 --> 00:30:13,021
9:08.

471
00:30:13,045 --> 00:30:16,324
So you didn't see everyone
who went into that room, did you?

472
00:30:16,348 --> 00:30:18,192
I guess not.

473
00:30:18,216 --> 00:30:19,461
If you missed one,
you could have

474
00:30:19,485 --> 00:30:20,695
missed another, couldn't you?

475
00:30:20,719 --> 00:30:21,830
I suppose.

476
00:30:21,854 --> 00:30:23,397
Like the real murderer.

477
00:30:23,421 --> 00:30:25,901
Objection! Withdraw the
question. Nothing further.

478
00:30:38,237 --> 00:30:40,237
Hey, here you go, Jimmy.

479
00:30:43,375 --> 00:30:45,007
Enjoy.

480
00:30:49,148 --> 00:30:51,058
Wait a minute. You
can't put me in here.

481
00:30:51,082 --> 00:30:52,594
Look, P.D. will be
down in about an hour.

482
00:30:52,618 --> 00:30:53,694
Look, I didn't do anything.

483
00:30:53,718 --> 00:30:55,585
The cops are just rousting me.

484
00:30:56,822 --> 00:30:58,332
You can't do this.

485
00:30:58,356 --> 00:30:59,968
Watch me.

486
00:31:07,065 --> 00:31:10,300
It's a small world, huh, Jimmy?

487
00:31:11,703 --> 00:31:12,780
Hey now, just wait a minute.

488
00:31:12,804 --> 00:31:14,950
You set me up.
It wasn't my idea.

489
00:31:14,974 --> 00:31:16,284
Then whose idea was it?!

490
00:31:16,308 --> 00:31:18,153
Hey, take it easy.
And you just back off!

491
00:31:18,177 --> 00:31:20,088
A name, Jimmy!

492
00:31:20,112 --> 00:31:21,256
Look, let him go.

493
00:31:23,916 --> 00:31:25,894
A name, Jimmy!

494
00:31:25,918 --> 00:31:29,231
I said, I want a name.

495
00:31:34,626 --> 00:31:35,992
Ah!

496
00:31:54,045 --> 00:31:55,045
Ugh.

497
00:32:05,624 --> 00:32:06,902
Guard. Guard!

498
00:32:06,926 --> 00:32:10,105
Your contact at the
Magic Castle set me up.

499
00:32:10,129 --> 00:32:11,639
Uh-huh.

500
00:32:11,663 --> 00:32:12,740
And I want a name, Jimmy.

501
00:32:12,764 --> 00:32:16,266
Arthur Wolfe is
who told me to do it.

502
00:32:17,269 --> 00:32:18,512
Get me out of here.

503
00:32:18,536 --> 00:32:20,681
Drop it.

504
00:32:20,705 --> 00:32:23,105
Hey. He attacked me.
It was self-defense.

505
00:32:24,543 --> 00:32:26,087
Look, you put him in the tank,

506
00:32:26,111 --> 00:32:29,012
and you get a doctor
up here right away.

507
00:32:32,718 --> 00:32:34,328
How'd I do? You were great.

508
00:32:34,352 --> 00:32:36,086
I got everything I need.

509
00:32:37,355 --> 00:32:40,134
Uh, Mr. Wolfe, uh...

510
00:32:40,158 --> 00:32:41,536
I wonder if you could help me

511
00:32:41,560 --> 00:32:43,938
with a little demonstration?

512
00:32:43,962 --> 00:32:47,108
Now, you're the manager
of the Magic Castle.

513
00:32:48,567 --> 00:32:50,678
Have you got a credit card
we could use for a minute?

514
00:32:50,702 --> 00:32:52,342
Sure. Thank you.

515
00:32:55,273 --> 00:32:56,985
Oh, that should do
fine. That should do fine.

516
00:32:57,009 --> 00:32:58,819
Just put it in my hand
there. Put it right there.

517
00:32:58,843 --> 00:33:00,288
All right. Okay.

518
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:02,756
All right, now, let's see here.

519
00:33:07,252 --> 00:33:10,286
Okay. You can have it back.

520
00:33:11,523 --> 00:33:13,001
All right.

521
00:33:13,025 --> 00:33:18,572
Now... there is no way
that I could have seen...

522
00:33:18,596 --> 00:33:21,575
the 10-digit number
on that card, is there?

523
00:33:21,599 --> 00:33:23,978
Uh, not that I could tell, no.

524
00:33:24,002 --> 00:33:26,180
Okay.

525
00:33:26,204 --> 00:33:28,849
All right. Now, let's see.

526
00:33:28,873 --> 00:33:34,978
It's, uh, 037...

527
00:33:38,550 --> 00:33:41,796
2326.

528
00:33:41,820 --> 00:33:43,164
Is that right?

529
00:33:43,188 --> 00:33:44,732
That's right.

530
00:33:44,756 --> 00:33:47,868
Objection, Your Honor.
This alleged demonstration

531
00:33:47,892 --> 00:33:49,237
is nothing more than a sideshow,

532
00:33:49,261 --> 00:33:52,307
the sole purpose of which
is to confuse and mislead

533
00:33:52,331 --> 00:33:54,108
the members of the jury.

534
00:33:54,132 --> 00:33:56,444
Your Honor, I'm using
this demonstration

535
00:33:56,468 --> 00:33:57,946
to establish motive.

536
00:33:57,970 --> 00:34:00,136
Overruled.

537
00:34:02,307 --> 00:34:05,686
Now, how'd I do that, Mr. Wolfe?

538
00:34:05,710 --> 00:34:08,189
Tell the court. I won't mind.

539
00:34:08,213 --> 00:34:09,590
To be honest, I don't know.

540
00:34:09,614 --> 00:34:11,292
I-I've seen scores of mentalists

541
00:34:11,316 --> 00:34:12,459
perform similar tricks,

542
00:34:12,483 --> 00:34:14,328
but, uh, they almost
always had assistants.

543
00:34:14,352 --> 00:34:17,632
Ah. And Sandy Farrell...

544
00:34:17,656 --> 00:34:21,469
was once the assistant
to Martel the Mentalist.

545
00:34:21,493 --> 00:34:22,770
Wasn't she? Yeah.

546
00:34:22,794 --> 00:34:26,407
And would you explain to
the court what the duties are

547
00:34:26,431 --> 00:34:28,264
of a mentalist's assistant?

548
00:34:30,135 --> 00:34:31,980
She holds up personal items

549
00:34:32,004 --> 00:34:34,416
belonging to members of the
audience. And the mentalist,

550
00:34:34,440 --> 00:34:36,884
even though he's blindfolded,
identifies the items correctly

551
00:34:36,908 --> 00:34:38,252
because of the code word

552
00:34:38,276 --> 00:34:39,888
she uses when she addresses him.

553
00:34:39,912 --> 00:34:42,190
As for your trick,
you've got me stumped.

554
00:34:44,683 --> 00:34:48,596
Well... I did it the
same way Sandy did it

555
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:50,931
when she was
Mr. Martel's assistant,

556
00:34:50,955 --> 00:34:54,301
and the same way the
current assistant does it.

557
00:34:54,325 --> 00:34:56,445
Well, you lost me there. Uh-huh.

558
00:34:57,862 --> 00:34:59,140
Putty.

559
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:02,811
It's putty.

560
00:35:02,835 --> 00:35:04,345
See?

561
00:35:04,369 --> 00:35:07,148
I have a perfect imprint...

562
00:35:07,172 --> 00:35:08,550
of that credit card number.

563
00:35:08,574 --> 00:35:10,919
See that?

564
00:35:10,943 --> 00:35:14,355
Only... Sandy...

565
00:35:14,379 --> 00:35:16,991
didn't use credit
cards, did she?

566
00:35:17,015 --> 00:35:20,495
She used... keys.

567
00:35:20,519 --> 00:35:22,263
Didn't she? I don't
know what you mean.

568
00:35:22,287 --> 00:35:24,532
At lease once a performance,

569
00:35:24,556 --> 00:35:28,770
Sandy would hold up
somebody's set of keys,

570
00:35:28,794 --> 00:35:33,908
and when nobody was looking,
she'd make a perfect imprint

571
00:35:33,932 --> 00:35:37,879
of one of those keys in putty.

572
00:35:37,903 --> 00:35:39,413
A house key.

573
00:35:39,437 --> 00:35:45,753
Then later, she'd take the
mold and have a real key made

574
00:35:45,777 --> 00:35:47,989
to somebody's house.

575
00:35:48,013 --> 00:35:49,824
Didn't she?

576
00:35:49,848 --> 00:35:51,326
How would I know?

577
00:35:51,350 --> 00:35:53,928
And even if that's so, she
was the one doing it. Not me.

578
00:35:53,952 --> 00:35:55,096
Heh.

579
00:35:55,120 --> 00:35:58,187
You're the manager
of the Magic Castle.

580
00:35:59,190 --> 00:36:00,868
You know all the members.

581
00:36:00,892 --> 00:36:02,503
Where their houses are.

582
00:36:02,527 --> 00:36:04,639
When they're in town,
when they're out of town.

583
00:36:04,663 --> 00:36:06,207
On trips.

584
00:36:06,231 --> 00:36:09,043
You're the one who
told the break-in man

585
00:36:09,067 --> 00:36:11,713
when and where
to go, didn't you?

586
00:36:11,737 --> 00:36:15,617
And Sandy was the one
who gave him the key,

587
00:36:15,641 --> 00:36:16,617
wasn't she?

588
00:36:16,641 --> 00:36:18,419
That's outrageous.

589
00:36:18,443 --> 00:36:21,989
I wonder what Jimmy
Collier would say

590
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:24,025
if I put him on the stand.

591
00:36:24,049 --> 00:36:25,660
What do you think he'd say?

592
00:36:25,684 --> 00:36:27,127
None of this means I killed her.

593
00:36:27,151 --> 00:36:28,896
I tell you what I think.

594
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:33,534
I think that even though
Sandy had moved on...

595
00:36:33,558 --> 00:36:36,504
and was now Danny
Fontaine's assistant,

596
00:36:36,528 --> 00:36:41,309
she still wanted her cut
of your break-in money.

597
00:36:41,333 --> 00:36:43,978
And after a while you
couldn't take that...

598
00:36:44,002 --> 00:36:45,647
so you got rid of
her, didn't you?

599
00:36:45,671 --> 00:36:47,414
I didn't kill her.

600
00:36:47,438 --> 00:36:49,216
You had motive and opportunity.

601
00:36:49,240 --> 00:36:51,552
I was on-stage when Sandy was...

602
00:36:51,576 --> 00:36:52,720
Rod Greenwood was on-stage.

603
00:36:52,744 --> 00:36:54,254
Not in the main
theater, in the bar.

604
00:36:54,278 --> 00:36:55,322
Remember, Mr. Matlock.

605
00:36:55,346 --> 00:36:56,790
My assistant stopped
you, remember?

606
00:36:56,814 --> 00:36:59,026
Wait, it's coming through,

607
00:36:59,050 --> 00:37:01,162
and somewhere there's
a bird looking for it.

608
00:37:01,186 --> 00:37:02,585
It's a feather.

609
00:37:04,355 --> 00:37:05,789
Oh, come on.

610
00:37:06,892 --> 00:37:08,502
That's right, me. No one knew.

611
00:37:08,526 --> 00:37:10,738
The owner, the other
performers. No one.

612
00:37:10,762 --> 00:37:12,507
I'm your Mr. Martel.

613
00:37:12,531 --> 00:37:15,042
I was on-stage when
Sandy was murdered,

614
00:37:15,066 --> 00:37:17,586
and I have a terrific
alibi, Mr. Matlock.

615
00:37:18,770 --> 00:37:20,414
You.

616
00:37:31,717 --> 00:37:33,149
No further questions.

617
00:37:52,670 --> 00:37:54,504
Hm-hm-hm-hm-hm-hm.

618
00:38:34,312 --> 00:38:35,344
Hm.

619
00:38:54,098 --> 00:38:56,365
Ah. M-Mr. Greenwood.

620
00:38:58,036 --> 00:39:01,348
Your act at the Magic Castle

621
00:39:01,372 --> 00:39:06,286
involves your climbing into
a trunk with handcuffs on,

622
00:39:06,310 --> 00:39:08,255
and your popping
out a few minutes later

623
00:39:08,279 --> 00:39:09,624
with handcuffs
off, is that right?

624
00:39:09,648 --> 00:39:12,493
That's right. Only my
feet are shackled too,

625
00:39:12,517 --> 00:39:14,261
and I change my clothes...

626
00:39:14,285 --> 00:39:17,253
in under two minutes.
Ooh. Hm. That's some trick.

627
00:39:18,490 --> 00:39:20,401
You... You mind telling
me how you do it?

628
00:39:21,426 --> 00:39:23,103
Yes, in fact I do.

629
00:39:23,127 --> 00:39:24,638
You're under oath.

630
00:39:24,662 --> 00:39:27,808
Keeping such things a secret
is essential to my livelihood.

631
00:39:27,832 --> 00:39:29,543
Surely you don't
expect me to sit here

632
00:39:29,567 --> 00:39:32,012
and destroy my own career?

633
00:39:32,036 --> 00:39:34,304
Hm.

634
00:39:35,640 --> 00:39:39,220
Uh, Your Honor, I
think I have a solution.

635
00:39:39,244 --> 00:39:41,522
If you'll allow
me to conjecture.

636
00:39:41,546 --> 00:39:45,359
And if I'm wrong...
Mr. Greenwood's secret

637
00:39:45,383 --> 00:39:48,162
can remain just that: a secret.

638
00:39:48,186 --> 00:39:49,797
All right. Proceed.

639
00:39:49,821 --> 00:39:53,267
Uh, but, Your Honor,
I have a problem.

640
00:39:53,291 --> 00:39:55,024
I can't do it. Heh.

641
00:39:56,127 --> 00:39:58,572
At least not here.

642
00:39:58,596 --> 00:40:02,176
Now, Mr. Greenwood,
is...? Is this your trunk?

643
00:40:02,200 --> 00:40:03,844
The one you used in your act

644
00:40:03,868 --> 00:40:05,713
the night Sandy
Farrell was murdered?

645
00:40:05,737 --> 00:40:06,747
Yes, it is.

646
00:40:06,771 --> 00:40:10,151
Well, what exactly
goes on during your act,

647
00:40:10,175 --> 00:40:11,519
from the audience's
point of view,

648
00:40:11,543 --> 00:40:14,255
I'm not asking you
to reveal any secrets.

649
00:40:14,279 --> 00:40:16,390
I pick up a volunteer
from the audience.

650
00:40:16,414 --> 00:40:19,360
Um, he or she comes
up on the stage,

651
00:40:19,384 --> 00:40:22,196
chains my hands and feet,
then closes me in the trunk.

652
00:40:22,220 --> 00:40:23,920
Okay. Tyler?

653
00:40:26,624 --> 00:40:27,735
We're gonna...

654
00:40:27,759 --> 00:40:29,670
We're gonna
reconstruct things here.

655
00:40:29,694 --> 00:40:32,172
Only this time I'll
be the volunteer,

656
00:40:32,196 --> 00:40:35,909
and Tyler Hudson here is
gonna play you, Mr. Greenwood.

657
00:40:35,933 --> 00:40:41,048
Now, let's... Let's pretend
that I've got Tyler all chained up.

658
00:40:41,072 --> 00:40:42,316
Now what happens?

659
00:40:42,340 --> 00:40:45,519
The volunteer opens the
lid, and I climb in the trunk.

660
00:40:45,543 --> 00:40:46,820
Okay.

661
00:40:46,844 --> 00:40:48,589
Tyler?

662
00:40:48,613 --> 00:40:50,658
Okay.

663
00:40:50,682 --> 00:40:51,958
Now what?

664
00:40:51,982 --> 00:40:54,263
Now the volunteer
closes the lid.

665
00:40:54,653 --> 00:40:57,387
Uh, then the volunteer
turns over the hourglass.

666
00:40:59,257 --> 00:41:01,269
The music plays,
the audience waits,

667
00:41:01,293 --> 00:41:03,371
and I extricate myself

668
00:41:03,395 --> 00:41:04,938
by the time the sand runs out.

669
00:41:04,962 --> 00:41:09,743
And all that time you're
wiggling out of those chains?

670
00:41:09,767 --> 00:41:12,287
And changing my clothes.

671
00:41:12,536 --> 00:41:16,149
All right, let's assume
the sand has run out.

672
00:41:16,173 --> 00:41:17,818
Then what?

673
00:41:17,842 --> 00:41:19,787
Then the volunteer opens
the lid, and I step out.

674
00:41:19,811 --> 00:41:20,811
Okay.

675
00:41:21,713 --> 00:41:22,993
Okay.

676
00:41:25,550 --> 00:41:27,428
Oh, I'll be "daggoned."

677
00:41:27,452 --> 00:41:30,298
How can you stand
up if you're not there?

678
00:41:30,322 --> 00:41:31,754
Tyler's gone.

679
00:41:33,158 --> 00:41:34,356
Tyler.

680
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:37,772
Mr. Matlock, what's
going on here?

681
00:41:37,796 --> 00:41:38,806
The same thing that went on

682
00:41:38,830 --> 00:41:40,763
the night of the
murder, Your Honor.

683
00:41:42,233 --> 00:41:44,945
You have two trunks,
don't you, Mr. Greenwood?

684
00:41:44,969 --> 00:41:47,081
One that you rehearse in

685
00:41:47,105 --> 00:41:49,016
and leave standing
around on the stage.

686
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:52,620
The other you keep
squirreled away downstairs.

687
00:41:52,644 --> 00:41:54,388
The one you use in your act.

688
00:41:54,412 --> 00:41:56,713
The one with the secret panel.

689
00:41:57,816 --> 00:41:59,460
This one. No,
this isn't my trunk.

690
00:41:59,484 --> 00:42:01,762
Mr. Greenwood, didn't you,

691
00:42:01,786 --> 00:42:03,998
the night of the murder,
get into this trunk,

692
00:42:04,022 --> 00:42:05,465
open the panel...

693
00:42:05,489 --> 00:42:07,701
slip through the trap
door on the stage,

694
00:42:07,725 --> 00:42:09,470
go to Sandy Farrell's
dressing room...

695
00:42:09,494 --> 00:42:11,472
No, of course not.

696
00:42:11,496 --> 00:42:13,607
Kill her with one of
Daniel Fontaine's knives?

697
00:42:13,631 --> 00:42:14,842
No. After you were finished,

698
00:42:14,866 --> 00:42:17,778
rush back to the trap
door, up into the trunk,

699
00:42:17,802 --> 00:42:20,114
just in time to come
out for your bow?

700
00:42:20,138 --> 00:42:21,549
That's impossible.

701
00:42:21,573 --> 00:42:23,517
For an accomplished
magician like yourself,

702
00:42:23,541 --> 00:42:25,353
you probably had time to spare.

703
00:42:25,377 --> 00:42:26,709
I was chained.

704
00:42:28,346 --> 00:42:31,626
Mr. Greenwood...

705
00:42:31,650 --> 00:42:35,896
are these the chains
you use in your act?

706
00:42:35,920 --> 00:42:37,687
Take a good look.

707
00:42:39,257 --> 00:42:40,468
I think so. Uh...

708
00:42:40,492 --> 00:42:43,670
All right. This time
you be the volunteer,

709
00:42:43,694 --> 00:42:45,706
and I'll get in the trunk.

710
00:42:45,730 --> 00:42:46,962
Put the cuffs on me.

711
00:42:49,367 --> 00:42:50,367
There you go.

712
00:42:52,237 --> 00:42:55,683
Okay, I'll step inside.

713
00:42:55,707 --> 00:42:57,384
Now put the chains on my feet.

714
00:43:03,214 --> 00:43:06,193
Okay. Now, I'll get down.

715
00:43:06,217 --> 00:43:08,963
And close the lid.

716
00:43:10,021 --> 00:43:13,722
And don't forget the hourglass.

717
00:43:22,133 --> 00:43:24,211
Ow. Ooh, ow.

718
00:43:24,235 --> 00:43:26,080
Ow. Wait, wait. Wait a minute.

719
00:43:26,104 --> 00:43:29,150
Wait. Wait a minute.
Oh, wait. Wait.

720
00:43:29,174 --> 00:43:31,106
Maybe this wasn't
such a good idea.

721
00:43:33,144 --> 00:43:35,211
How'd I do that?

722
00:43:37,115 --> 00:43:43,898
The same way you did
the night of the murder.

723
00:43:43,922 --> 00:43:44,932
With this.

724
00:43:44,956 --> 00:43:46,366
Now, that isn't my key.

725
00:43:46,390 --> 00:43:50,170
Mr. Greenwood, those are
your chains and your locks.

726
00:43:50,194 --> 00:43:52,173
This must be your key.

727
00:43:52,197 --> 00:43:54,963
'Cause I found it right
where you dropped it.

728
00:43:56,133 --> 00:43:58,000
Let me show you something.

729
00:44:03,875 --> 00:44:06,709
Looks like something you'd
take up collection with, doesn't it?

730
00:44:07,879 --> 00:44:11,192
This is a magic bag.

731
00:44:11,216 --> 00:44:12,216
Look at that.

732
00:44:19,490 --> 00:44:21,323
Where'd it go?

733
00:44:32,470 --> 00:44:34,147
It's got a secret compartment.

734
00:44:34,171 --> 00:44:35,938
See that.

735
00:44:38,710 --> 00:44:43,857
It was on the floor by
Sandy Farrell's body.

736
00:44:43,881 --> 00:44:46,649
I found your key...

737
00:44:48,953 --> 00:44:51,065
in the secret compartment.

738
00:44:51,089 --> 00:44:53,500
I gotta figure the night

739
00:44:53,524 --> 00:44:56,170
you murdered her,
this compartment

740
00:44:56,194 --> 00:45:00,407
was partly open, and
during the struggle...

741
00:45:00,431 --> 00:45:01,742
somehow,

742
00:45:01,766 --> 00:45:07,081
thousand chances to one...
A thousand changes to one

743
00:45:07,105 --> 00:45:13,086
when you dropped it, it fell
in the secret compartment.

744
00:45:13,110 --> 00:45:15,622
You didn't have a lot
of time to look for it.

745
00:45:15,646 --> 00:45:18,024
You didn't know anyone
would find it, or if they did,

746
00:45:18,048 --> 00:45:19,660
wouldn't appreciate
its significance.

747
00:45:19,684 --> 00:45:21,962
Maybe you didn't
know you lost it.

748
00:45:21,986 --> 00:45:24,465
That doesn't prove
anything. I have lots of keys.

749
00:45:24,489 --> 00:45:27,034
Then why did you
go to the locksmith

750
00:45:27,058 --> 00:45:29,770
the very next morning...

751
00:45:29,794 --> 00:45:31,394
and have another key made?

752
00:45:32,630 --> 00:45:35,264
One that would fit those locks.

753
00:45:39,169 --> 00:45:42,037
You did go to the locksmith.

754
00:45:44,475 --> 00:45:46,687
Didn't you?

755
00:45:56,254 --> 00:46:00,901
We find the defendant,
Daniel Fontaine, not guilty.

756
00:46:00,925 --> 00:46:03,937
The jury having found
the defendant not guilty,

757
00:46:03,961 --> 00:46:06,139
this court is adjourned.

758
00:46:07,164 --> 00:46:08,408
Congratulations.

759
00:46:08,432 --> 00:46:09,409
Well, uh, thank you.

760
00:46:09,433 --> 00:46:11,111
Uh, there you go.

761
00:46:11,135 --> 00:46:12,267
Thank you.

762
00:46:15,372 --> 00:46:17,251
Mr. Fontaine, over here, please.

763
00:46:17,275 --> 00:46:19,219
Sir, can I get one
shot over here.

764
00:46:19,243 --> 00:46:20,963
Thank you.

765
00:46:22,213 --> 00:46:24,253
Miss, may I get a picture?

766
00:46:27,551 --> 00:46:28,728
You all right?

767
00:46:28,752 --> 00:46:30,964
I'm always all right.

768
00:46:30,988 --> 00:46:32,900
Well, you put on a fine fight.

769
00:46:32,924 --> 00:46:35,502
Well, that's show business.

770
00:46:35,526 --> 00:46:36,526
Oh.

771
00:46:37,695 --> 00:46:40,808
So in celebration of
yet another victory,

772
00:46:40,832 --> 00:46:43,243
I'm gonna take you to dinner.

773
00:46:43,267 --> 00:46:44,544
Yeah? Yeah.

774
00:46:44,568 --> 00:46:46,936
Then I'm gonna
make you disappear.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
